    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "Henri Farman Breaks the\nOne-Minute Barrier\nNovember 9, 1907 • Paris, France\n50C RWANDA\nVOISIN N° 1 bis (1908)\n2,10\nPHILA\nEPUBLIQUE FRANÇAIS\n9 XI 1985\nPARIS",
      "International Iributes to the History of Flight\n= Henri Farman Breaks the One-Minute Barrier =\nA former artist and racecar driver, Henri Farman pur-\nin flight. Among the extensive modifications Farman\nchased his tirst powered aircratt, a Voisin biplane, in\nmade to the plane before his milestone flight were\nthe early 1900's. Then he set about to make major\nthe removal of the vertical panels between the wings\nalterations to it, thus developing the aircraft that be-\nand the improvement of the tail surfaces and land-\ncame known as the Voisin-Farman I.\ning gear.\nAfter a series of successful tests, Farman took off\nin his biplane from Issy-les-Moulineaux, outside of\nParis, on November 9, 1907. As the amazed onlookers\ncheered and waved, the plane completed a circuit of\n3,380 feet. Even more important, it stayed in the air\nMint Stamp: The mint stamp on this Cover was issued\nby Rwanda in 1978. It shows Henri Farman and his\nVoisin-Farman I in which he achieved another mile-\nstone in 1908 — the first officially logged flight of\nmore than one kilometer.\nfor a full 74 seconds - 15 seconds longer than the\nprevious duration record set by the Wright brothers\nin 1903. The one-minute barrier was broken at last.\nThe full-color cachet pictures the Voisin-Farman I\nPostmark: This Cover was postmarked in Paris, France\non November 9, 1985, the exact anniversary of the\nfirst European flight of more than one minute.\n© Postal Commemorative Society\nPrinted in U.S.A.\n1019.056.012"
    ],
